@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+# Rollback instructions
+
+## Before applying
+
+- Export a Limitless backup from the current production app.
+- Create a repository backup or Git tag.
+- Back up the Supabase database.
+- Keep the existing Vercel deployment available for rollback.
+
+## Source rollback
+
+If the complete-project copy was committed, revert that commit through the
+repository owner's normal Git workflow and redeploy the prior source.
+
+If `handoff/apply-checkpoint.mjs` was used, it prints the backup directory and
+writes an `apply-report.json` there. Restore each backed-up path from that
+directory and remove only paths listed as newly created in the report. Review
+the report before deleting anything.
+
+## Browser data rollback
+
+Do not clear site data. V2 keeps the legacy source record and recovery
+snapshots. Export a v2 backup before returning to the older deployment. The
+older app may ignore new v2 records, but clearing IndexedDB would remove the
+rollback evidence.
+
+## Supabase rollback
+
+The v2 migration is additive, and older application code ignores its RPC.
+Leaving the function installed is safer than dropping it during an emergency
+source rollback. If policy requires removal, first back up the database, stop
+v2 clients, review active calls, and have the database owner explicitly approve
+the SQL change. Do not drop application tables, storage objects, or user rows.
+
+## Environment rollback
+
+The two Supabase public variables can remain for the prior sync-capable app.
+Remove the optional Pollinations variables if that route must be disabled; a
+run budget of `0` also keeps it off. Never replace a public variable with a
+secret key.
